What CC BY 4.0 lets you do
--------------------------
  share        copy and redistribute the content in any medium or format
  adapt        remix, transform, and build upon it, including commercially

  under one condition: **attribution**. You must give appropriate credit,
  provide a link to the license, and indicate if changes were made.

How to attribute
----------------
A form like this is sufficient:

  Based on QuantCordis (https://github.com/QuantCordis/QuantCordis),
  licensed under CC BY 4.0. Changes were made.

Why split the licenses
----------------------
The rule list and research notes are the part of this project meant to be read,
quoted, and reused in other people's methodology — CC BY is written for prose
and asks for attribution, which is what we want there.

The code is meant to be run and shipped inside other software — MIT is written
for that, with the warranty disclaimer software needs. CC BY is not suitable
for source code, and MIT is weak at expressing attribution for prose.

Code snippets inside documents
------------------------------
Short code samples that appear *inside* a .md file may be copied and used under
the MIT License, so that reading a document never puts a license obstacle in the
way of using the code it explains.

If you would rather that adaptations of the written content stay open, the
alternative is CC BY-SA 4.0; that is a one-line change to this file, at the
copyright holder's discretion.

